---
title: "Theming"
description: "Restyling a document by overriding a handful of variables, and why the light/dark choice belongs to the reader's device rather than to either of you."
---

A theme in Taliesin is not a stylesheet you wrestle with, it is a small set of CSS
custom properties (`--tali-*`). The whole document, the navbar, footer, callouts,
diagrams, figures, and syntax-highlighted code all read those variables, so
retuning one value restyles everything at once, and the light/dark switch is just
a swap of the values: nothing re-renders.

Not everything is a variable. Prose also gets quiet typographic polish that needs no
configuration: balanced headings and captions, pretty last-line wrapping, and figures
that keep their caption attached.

## The built-in themes

Two themes ship in the binary and need no files:

- **`light`** is the base `:root` palette: a white page with dark ink.
- **`dark`** overrides the colour variables for a near-black page with light ink.

**Neither is selected in front matter, and there is no key for it.** Both ship in every
page and the reader's device decides which one paints, following the OS
`prefers-color-scheme` and falling back to light when the OS expresses no preference.
`theme:` itself is very much alive, for the custom `.css`/`.scss` files and
`_extensions/` bundles described below.

::: {.callout-note}
## Two built-in palettes, and the device picks between them

Light and Dark are the whole built-in set. A warm low-contrast "sepia" palette
shipped for a while and was removed: a third palette is a third of every rule and
every contrast measurement, for a preference a reader can already get from their
system. Which of the two a reader sees follows their device and nothing else; see
[Reader controls](../reference/accessibility.tmd#reader-controls) for why the picker
that used to sit in the top-right corner went away.
:::

## How the switch works

It comes down to one attribute, `data-theme` on `<html>`, and CSS does the rest.
The base palette lives in a `:root` block and `html[data-theme="dark"]` supplies
override values for the same `--tali-*` names.
A pre-paint `<head>` script reads the device preference before the first frame and sets
that attribute, so a reader never sees a flash of the wrong palette, and it keeps listening
so a mid-read change to the OS setting flips the page live. Crucially,
**syntax highlighting recolours with no re-highlight**: code is highlighted once,
server-side, into scope classes (`tali-hl-keyword`, `tali-hl-string`, …), and the dark
theme simply restyles those classes, so a toggle is pure CSS with nothing to
recompute. The same holds for math, figures (a light/dark image swap), and callouts:
all **fully offline**, the colours baked into the page, no CDN and no client re-run.

**Diagrams are the one exception.** Mermaid bakes its colours into the rendered SVG, so
a diagram cannot be recoloured by CSS alone: it listens for the theme change (the
`tali:themechange` event) and re-runs from its stashed source under the new palette. The
library itself is vendored like everything else, so this costs no network either.

## The variable reference

Every knob a theme can turn, with its light and dark values.

### Colours

| Variable | Light | Dark | Role |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| `--tali-bg` | `#ffffff` | `#16181d` | Page background |
| `--tali-fg` | `#1a1a1a` | `#e6e6e6` | Body text |
| `--tali-muted` | `#555` | `#9aa0aa` | Secondary / metadata text |
| `--tali-accent` | `#4c8dff` | `#6ea8ff` | Accent (hover, focus ring, active markers) |
| `--tali-accent-fill` | `#1f6feb` | `#2563eb` | Filled active/pressed surfaces |
| `--tali-on-accent` | `#ffffff` | `#ffffff` | Text on a filled accent surface |
| `--tali-link` | `#2563eb` | `#6ea8ff` | Links |
| `--tali-code-bg` | `#f5f5f5` | `#21242b` | Code-block / inline-code background |
| `--tali-border` | `#e3e3e3` | `#363a44` | Hairlines, card + control borders |
| `--tali-edge-shadow` | `rgba(0,0,0,.16)` | `rgba(255,255,255,.14)` | Floating-element shadow |
| `--tali-flash` | `rgba(76,141,255,.18)` | `rgba(110,168,255,.22)` | Live-edit pulse on a re-rendered block |

::: {.callout-note}
## Why two accent variables

The bare `--tali-accent` fails WCAG-AA contrast with white text, so a filled surface
(a segmented control, a pressed button, the primary call-to-action) uses the darker
`--tali-accent-fill` for the fill and `--tali-on-accent` for its text; both clear
4.5:1 in each theme. If you override the accent, override `--tali-accent-fill` to a
matching darker shade too.
:::

### Callout accents

One value per callout kind sets that callout's border and icon colour; the
title-bar tint is derived from it with `color-mix`, so light and dark both work
from a single definition (no per-kind dark override needed).

| Variable | Light value | Kind |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `--tali-callout-note` | `#4c8dff` | note |
| `--tali-callout-tip` | `#2bb673` | tip |
| `--tali-callout-warning` | `#e0a800` | warning |

### Fonts, layout, and focus

| Variable | Default | Role |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `--tali-font-body` | `1.0625rem/1.7 ui-serif, Georgia, …, serif` | Body font (size, leading, stack) |
| `--tali-font-head` | `ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif` | Heading + UI font |
| `--tali-font-mono` | `ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace` | Code font |
| `--tali-maxw` | `46rem` | Reading-column max width |
| `--tali-scale` | `1.2` | Modular type-scale ratio for heading sizes (a minor third) |
| `--tali-focus` | `2px solid var(--tali-accent)` | Keyboard-focus ring for every control |
| `--tali-focus-offset` | `2px` | Offset of that ring |

::: {.callout-tip}
## One token, every control

`--tali-focus` is the single focus ring for links, buttons, tabs, the reader menu,
and the reader controls, applied via `:focus-visible`. Retune it once and every
interactive element follows.
:::

### Diagram colours (Mermaid)

Mermaid bakes its colours into the SVG when it renders, so they cannot be
restyled with ordinary CSS. Instead Taliesin reads the diagram's config from a
second set of CSS variables and re-renders the diagram on a light/dark switch, so
you theme diagrams from the same theme file, with no JavaScript.

| Variable | Role | Default |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `--tali-mermaid-theme` | Mermaid theme name (`default`, `dark`, `neutral`, `forest`, `base`) | `dark` in dark mode, else `default` |
| `--tali-mermaid-bg` | Diagram background | the Mermaid theme's |
| `--tali-mermaid-node` | Node fill | the Mermaid theme's |
| `--tali-mermaid-node-border` | Node border | the Mermaid theme's |
| `--tali-mermaid-text` | Node text | the Mermaid theme's |
| `--tali-mermaid-line` | Edges and arrows | the Mermaid theme's |

Set nothing and diagrams follow the built-in light and dark themes. For full
colour control, choose `--tali-mermaid-theme: base` (Mermaid's `base` theme is the
one built to be customised) and point the colour variables at the rest of your
palette:

```css
html[data-theme="dark"] {
  --tali-mermaid-theme: base;
  --tali-mermaid-bg: var(--tali-bg);
  --tali-mermaid-node: var(--tali-code-bg);
  --tali-mermaid-node-border: var(--tali-border);
  --tali-mermaid-text: var(--tali-fg);
  --tali-mermaid-line: var(--tali-muted);
}
```

The zoom (click-to-enlarge) backdrop uses `--tali-bg`, so an enlarged diagram
matches the page.

### An `.svg` file you wrote yourself

A figure written as `![Caption](diagram.svg)` is embedded as `<img src="diagram.svg">`,
and an `<img>` is **style-isolated**: the SVG is a separate document, so nothing inside it
can see the page's `--tali-*` variables or the `data-theme` attribute the toggle sets.
This is a browser rule, not a Taliesin one. Inline `{js}` graphics and Mermaid diagrams
are part of the page and follow the toggle normally; an `<img>`-embedded SVG cannot.

The consequence worth knowing: an SVG that themes itself with

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { … }
```

follows the reader's **operating system**, not the page. For most readers those agree. For
a reader who has deliberately set the page to light while their OS is dark, they disagree,
and the figure comes out in the wrong palette on an otherwise correct page.

Two ways to write a figure that is right either way:

- **Use a palette that works on both.** Mid-tone strokes and fills, chosen to hold contrast
  against a light and a dark background alike, and no `prefers-color-scheme` block. This is
  the cheapest option and the one to reach for by default. `corpus/demo-book/structure.svg`
  is written this way: `#888` strokes, no `prefers-color-scheme` block anywhere in it.
- **Draw it in the page instead.** A `{js}` cell (or a Mermaid diagram) renders into the
  document, reads `--tali-*` directly, and re-renders on `tali:themechange`. Right for a
  figure whose colours genuinely have to match the page.

::: {.callout-note}
An `<style>` block inside an `.svg` file is parsed as **XML**, not as CDATA. A bare `<`,
`>` or `&` anywhere in it — including inside a CSS comment — makes the file malformed, and
a malformed SVG still serves with a `200` and still copies into the build while rendering
as nothing. Escape them, or keep the CSS free of those characters.
:::

## Three ways to customize

From lightest touch to a full bundle.

### a. Override a few variables for a whole project

Put a `:root` block in your project's `_site.yml` `head:`. Because the overrides land
*after* the base stylesheet, they win:

```yaml
# _site.yml
head:
  text: "<style>:root { --tali-accent: #d6336c; --tali-accent-fill: #a61e4d; }</style>"
```

To recolour the dark theme too, override the same names inside
`html[data-theme="dark"] { … }`.

There is no per-document `css:` key. It was removed on 2026-08-02 (nothing used it), and
the two remaining routes are better shaped for the job: `head:` for a project-wide tweak,
and `theme:` below for anything longer than a couple of lines.

### b. Point `theme:` at a `.css` or `.scss` file

For more than a couple of lines, keep the overrides in a file beside the document
and name it in `theme:`. The file is inlined after the base stylesheet, and on a
preview edit the dev server hot-swaps it in place with no reload:

```yaml
---
title: "My post"
theme: brand.css
---
```

```css
/* brand.css (beside the .tmd) */
:root {
  --tali-accent: #d6336c;
  --tali-accent-fill: #a61e4d;
  --tali-font-body: 1.0625rem/1.7 "Iowan Old Style", Georgia, serif;
  --tali-maxw: 50rem;
}
html[data-theme="dark"] {
  --tali-accent: #f06595;
  --tali-accent-fill: #c2255c;
}
```

A `.css`/`.scss` path that can't be read is flagged as a warning (a likely typo).
The path must stay inside the project: an absolute path or one escaping the root is
refused.

There is no Sass compiler. A `.scss` file is read and inlined verbatim inside a
`<style>` block, exactly like a `.css` file, so only plain-CSS-compatible content
works: real SCSS syntax (nesting, `$variables`, `@mixin`, `@use`) will not parse. The
`.scss` extension is accepted only so a file named that way is still found and inlined.

### c. Ship a `_extensions/<name>/` theme bundle

To reuse a theme across documents, a whole book, or a site, package it as a theme
extension: a folder `_extensions/<name>/` whose `theme.css` holds the palette.
Select it by bare name:

```yaml
---
title: "My post"
theme: brand
---
```

The `theme.css` is inlined exactly like a plain `.css` file, so a bundle is simply a
reusable place to keep a palette, selected by name from any document, book, or site.

## A worked example

Override the accent colour and the body font, in both light and dark, with a
two-line front-matter block and a small file. Drop `brand.css` beside your `.tmd`:

```css
/* brand.css */
:root {
  --tali-accent: #e8590c;            /* warm orange accent */
  --tali-accent-fill: #d9480f;       /* darker fill keeps white text AA-legible */
  --tali-font-body: 1.0625rem/1.7 "Charter", Georgia, serif;
}
html[data-theme="dark"] {
  --tali-accent: #ff922b;
  --tali-accent-fill: #e8590c;
}
```

```yaml
---
title: "My post"
theme: brand.css
---
```

Now links, the focus ring, and the active markers are orange, the body sets in
Charter, and both themes stay contrast-safe. Nothing else in the document needed
touching, and the page still swaps light and dark instantly.
